{"parentUuid":"2da05d41-c40c-4065-ac65-f4be328b8034","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwShke9y7Be1h6LmaqcE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GWgqQorUS6aT8NBJFKD1dU","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"bug-hunter","model":"sonnet","name":"release-safety-audit","description":"Audit stale release PR safety","prompt":"Read-only audit, do not edit files. Repo: Callisto (a release tool that publishes to crates.io when a bot-created release PR merges). Trail #162 (\"A stale release PR can no longer wedge releases\") described an incident: a transient GitHub error left the release PR stale, it was merged, and every later push failed the Release workflow with E165 \"no changeset was consumed by this commit\". The trail's plan was: (1) revert the bad merge (done long ago), (2) retry the forge calls in .github/actions/callisto-action/scripts/create-or-update-release-pr.sh with bounded backoff and stop swallowing stderr, (3) add a pre-merge guard: a check on the release PR that fails when the merge result still has pending changesets or the PR was built from a changeset set that differs from main (specified in docs/specs/release.json first), (4) document the E165 recovery in the release docs and decide whether `callisto release pending` should name it, (5) a `callisto status` warning about stray tags like `callisto-graph@0`.\nTask: for each of the five items, determine from the CURRENT code on origin/main whether it is done, partly done or missing, with evidence (file and line, spec criterion ids, tests, docs sections). Read: the script above, .github/workflows/callisto-release.yml and callisto-hold-release-pr.yml, docs/specs/release.json (REL-PR-* criteria), docs/releasing.md, docs/errors.md E165, the tests under .github/tests and .github/actions/callisto-action/tests, and the git log of those files (git log --oneline -- <path>). Also answer: today, can a stale release PR (built from a changeset set that differs from main) be merged without any check failing before the merge? What exactly stops it, and what would a user see? Look for the \"Hold release PR\" workflow's behavior and the `release-candidate` job's verification. Report findings only: status per item, the single highest-risk gap if any, and a concrete minimal design for any missing guard that respects these repo rules: Callisto features go in Rust, not shell; the workflow policy check forbids new secrets and write permissions outside the publish job; keep it lean. Under 500 words; no padding."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":840026,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":7660,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1372},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":840026,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7660,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":840026,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":840026},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"}],"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"previous_message_not_found"}},"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GWgqQorUS6aT8NBJFKD1dU":{"subagent_type":"bug-hunter","model":"sonnet","name":"release-safety-audit","description":"Audit stale release PR safety","prompt":"Read-only audit, do not edit files. Repo: Callisto (a release tool that publishes to crates.io when a bot-created release PR merges). Trail #162 (\"A stale release PR can no longer wedge releases\") described an incident: a transient GitHub error left the release PR stale, it was merged, and every later push failed the Release workflow with E165 \"no changeset was consumed by this commit\". The trail's plan was: (1) revert the bad merge (done long ago), (2) retry the forge calls in .github/actions/callisto-action/scripts/create-or-update-release-pr.sh with bounded backoff and stop swallowing stderr, (3) add a pre-merge guard: a check on the release PR that fails when the merge result still has pending changesets or the PR was built from a changeset set that differs from main (specified in docs/specs/release.json first), (4) document the E165 recovery in the release docs and decide whether `callisto release pending` should name it, (5) a `callisto status` warning about stray tags like `callisto-graph@0`.\nTask: for each of the five items, determine from the CURRENT code on origin/main whether it is done, partly done or missing, with evidence (file and line, spec criterion ids, tests, docs sections). Read: the script above, .github/workflows/callisto-release.yml and callisto-hold-release-pr.yml, docs/specs/release.json (REL-PR-* criteria), docs/releasing.md, docs/errors.md E165, the tests under .github/tests and .github/actions/callisto-action/tests, and the git log of those files (git log --oneline -- <path>). Also answer: today, can a stale release PR (built from a changeset set that differs from main) be merged without any check failing before the merge? What exactly stops it, and what would a user see? Look for the \"Hold release PR\" workflow's behavior and the `release-candidate` job's verification. Report findings only: status per item, the single highest-risk gap if any, and a concrete minimal design for any missing guard that respects these repo rules: Callisto features go in Rust, not shell; the workflow policy check forbids new secrets and write permissions outside the publish job; keep it lean. Under 500 words; no padding."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"5cd8af54-84ad-48e2-ae3e-10ac41280718","apiBlockIndex":6,"requestId":"req_01