{"parentUuid":"d1b65bf8-f5c7-4cba-a291-d003890358fb","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwZnVN6iHnxybfUEaw2U","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72,"cache_read_input_tokens":963254,"output_tokens":2391,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":249},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2391,"cache_read_input_tokens":963254,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":2376,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"c4871bae-701e-45e8-9aca-d1c79630004b","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgwZnV1G3Zdyatwrh3X8e","requestRef":"a4c8d965-eca0-40ab-9db9-858ea3661c43","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"92a0c4a8-811c-453b-ae2d-756d11093201","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:51:56.612Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"92a0c4a8-811c-453b-ae2d-756d11093201","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwZnVN6iHnxybfUEaw2U","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's a prompt you can hand to a read-only audit agent. I'd run it with `bug-hunter` on Sonnet, since it needs to read code and make judgment calls, and the results should come back ranked and with evidence, not as edits.\n\n````markdown\n# Task: find over-engineering and non-idiomatic Rust in Callisto, read-only\n\nRepo: `orin-dx/callisto` (a synchronous Rust release CLI, no async runtime). Do not edit files. Read AGENTS.md and docs/stability.md first.\n\n## Why\n\nA recent batch of work shipped machinery where the compiler or a smaller design would do. The owner's principle: in Rust, enforce invariants with types, traits and exhaustive matches so the compiler checks them; a test is the fallback for what types cannot see, and it should be small. Delete before adding. Every public surface (an error code, a JSON key, a flag) is permanent after 1.0.\n\n## What to look for (concrete patterns we already found, so find more of them)\n\n1. **Stringly-typed classification.** A hand-maintained list of strings that mirrors code (for example `USAGE_CODES` and a 233-code `RUNTIME_CODES` string in `crates/callisto-cli/tests/usage_exit_codes_tests.rs` deciding exit codes by matching error-code strings). Look for: matching on `code()` strings, `&[&str]` lists of names that parallel an enum, string comparisons where an enum or a trait method belongs.\n2. **Tests that re-check what the type system guarantees.** A test that runs many binaries to assert a property a trait bound already enforces (for example `emit_report<R: Report>` already requires a schema version). Look for tests whose failure is impossible without a compile error first.\n3. **Source-text scanning tests.** `include_str!`/`read_to_string` of `src/**` followed by string search. This repo deleted one before (#488). Also tests that grep docs or workflow files for strings that code could expose directly.\n4. **Shell or text files where Rust works.** Scripts under `.github/tests`, `justfile` recipes, or `.txt` fixtures that compare data a Rust test could check with crates already in the tree (`insta`, `yaml-rust2`, `toml`, `serde_json`). Shell is right only for testing shell.\n5. **Speculative generality.** Code for unsupported platforms (docs/stability.md says Windows is unsupported), hypothetical limits, configuration or parameters with one caller and one value, traits with one implementor and no test double, options nobody sets.\n6. **Global mutable state** (`static` with `Mutex`/`OnceLock`) where returning data would do.\n7. **Wall-clock or timing assertions** in tests. The repo's pattern is counting calls with a recording runner.\n8. **Copies instead of a shared abstraction.** The same enum-to-string, error-to-exit-code or struct-to-JSON mapping written at several sites; many near-identical tests that a table (`rstest`) or one generic helper would replace.\n9. **Surface area that need not be permanent.** Public error codes, JSON keys, flags or config keys added one-per-condition where a broader one with the detail in the message would do. Count how many exist per area.\n10. **Wildcard arms and flag soup.** `_ =>` arms on our own enums, `bool` parameters or several booleans that can contradict (should be an enum), `Option` fields that are only valid together (should be one enum or struct), `String` where a newtype or validated type belongs (ids, paths, tags, SHAs).\n11. **Reinvented crate functionality.** Hand-written parsing, edit distance, glob matching, retry, path normalisation or CLI handling where a crate already in `Cargo.toml` does it. Check `Cargo.lock` before claiming a crate is unavailable; do not propose adding a dependency unless the code is large and clearly worse.\n12. **Comments, docs and tests that restate code** or narrate history (AGENTS.md forbids history comments).\n\n## What is NOT a finding (refute before reporting)\n\n- A test that drives the real binary to check observable behavior (exit code, stderr, files written, JSON shape). Those are the right kind.\n- Code that is required by a documented 1.0 stability promise in docs/stability.md.\n- Durability, safety or security invariants in AGENTS.md (for example atomic writes with fsync).\n- A trait with several real implementors, or a test double that a test actually uses.\n- Anything you cannot show is reachable or duplicated: read the callers.\n\n## Method\n\nWork area by area (`callisto-model`, `callisto-manifests`, `callisto-graph`, `callisto-cli`, `crates/*/tests`, `.github/tests`, `justfile`). For each candidate: read the code and its callers, then count (how many sites, how many lines of test versus production, how many callers). Use the repo's own tooling where cheap (`rg`, `cargo tree`, `wc -l`); do not run the full test suite.\n\n## Report\n\nRank findings by **value of removing or simplifying** (lines removed, surface reduced, bug class made impossible), highest first. For each:\n- Pattern number, file and line, and how many instances exist.\n- Evidence (the caller you read, the count you took, the guarantee the type already gives).\n- The lean replacement in one or two sentences, naming the type, trait, crate or deletion.\n- Risk of the change and whether it touches a frozen 1.0 surface.\n- Confidence (high, medium, low).\n\nEnd with the five highest-value changes and a list of patterns you looked for and did not find. Under 800 words. Do not pad, and do not propose new features or new tests unless a deletion needs one.\n````\n\n**Why it's built this way**\n- **Concrete patterns from our own findings**, so the agent has real examples to match against instead of generic advice.\n- **A \"not a finding\" section**, because over-eager audits flag good tests and documented promises.\n- **Counts and callers required**, so each finding has evidence and I can verify it before acting.\n- **Ranked by value of removal**, with the lean replacement named, so the output turns straight into a short list of deletions.\n\n**How I'd use the result:** read the ranked list, check the top items myself against the code (as I did with the earlier audits, whose first inventory missed a case), and then batch the approved deletions into one or two small PRs.\n\nneeds input: say go and I'll run it with `bug-hunter` on Sonnet and bring you the ranked list."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72,"cache_read_input_tokens":963254,"output_tokens":2391,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":249},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2391,"cache_read_input_tokens":963254,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"c4871bae-701e-45e8-9aca-d1c79630004b","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgwZnV1G3Zdyatwrh3X8e","requestRef":"a4c8d965-eca0-40ab-9db9-858ea3661c43","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"258fc0b1-f81e-44bc-a060-7b76b9592a65","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:52:14.667Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-s