{"parentUuid":"02f220cb-006c-48b5-ad84-dc82df57df20","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d63a3721-2a40-4bbf-88c4-736158ed03ba","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<system-reminder>\n<tools-used-before-summary>Agent, Artifact, Bash, Edit, EnterWorktree, Grep, ListAgents, Monitor, Read, SendFeedback, SendMessage, SubagentHandback, ToolSearch, WebSearch, Write</tools-used-before-summary>\n</system-reminder>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - Long-running Callisto work (Rust release tool, `orin-dx/callisto`): world-class release PR and changelog, 1.0 stability hardening, performance, process improvement, with agent teams used in parallel at controlled cost.\n   - Current standing user directives: always sign commits and never override signing; one PR per independent topic off `origin/main` (no stacks); name worktrees for the task; never put claude.ai session links or Claude-Session trailers in commits or PRs; judge checks by exit code; PR/commit text succinct, one line per paragraph, no hard wrap; Entire auto-push is intended; use courier/smith/other plugin agents by default (the user was angry I wrote changesets freehand); Callisto features belong in Rust not shell; no Claude hooks; do not edit global `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` without approval (a \"Verifying work\" rule was approved and added); Entire blocks trail comments from Claude sessions, so record decisions in trail descriptions.\n   - Changeset standard (user-driven): say what is new, why it matters, what to do; name the visible thing; layered shape (lead line, audience sections such as What you see / Scripting / Upgrading, bullets and sub-bullets); no hard length targets; one entry per user-visible outcome; written in the PR that makes it visible; none for fixes to unreleased features or internal/test/doc-only work; fresh cold-reader agent check; read the rendered release PR before merging.\n   - Direction for the next release: \"Let's include more of our improvements, stability, performance work into the next version\" (so #493, the 0.20.0 release PR, stays unmerged until the batch lands); \"Keep going on feature work towards v1\".\n   - Latest user messages: (a) asked whether the Action-input shell/text-file test and the schema-version output test were idiomatic Rust (\"Is this the best way to setup these tests? Shell scripts, text files, etc?\"; \"Is the whole schema version output test also idiomatic and best practice? Is there no better way to do this?\"); (b) \"That is exactly what I'm thinking. Rust is structure for compiler checks, that's why the compile is slow. Seems like you're not thinking in the rust way and you're over-engineering way too much\"; (c) \"What other places are we making similar mistakes or other mistakes that cause over-engineering and waste?\"; (d) \"What can we change about rust guidance to prefer types, traits, usage of common crates for specific cases (like tokio, miete, etc)\"; (e) the final request: \"What kind of prompt and guidance would you give to another agent to look for similar patterns of issues?\" (text-only answer; this summary stands in for that turn).\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Typed JSON output: `emit_report<R: callisto_model::Report>` (envelope with `schemaVersion`, `command`, `dryRun`); untyped `write_json` was the escape hatch, now split into crate-private `write_document` and `pub fn write_error_json`.\n   - serde: `rename_all` on an enum renames variants only; `rename_all_fields = \"camelCase\"` renames the fields; `deny_unknown_fields`; `#[serde(default, skip_serializing_if)]` for additive fields.\n   - Exit codes and error codes: `docs/stability.md` promises 0 success, 1 failure, 2 usage; `USAGE_CODES`/`USAGE_WHEN_FROM_FLAG_CODES` in `crates/callisto-cli/src/error.rs` plus a `RUNTIME_CODES` string in `tests/usage_exit_codes_tests.rs`; codes are permanent after 1.0 (never reused).\n   - Git performance: one `git diff` per tag commit (`changed_source_files_since_last_tag`), `PATHSPEC_BUDGET`, batched `first_parent_commits_by_path` using `--first-parent -m` (not `--diff-merges`, which needs git 2.31), `ReleaseOwners` map replacing the per-entry `workspace_package` rescan, `RecordingGit`-style call-count tests, macOS `F_FULLFSYNC` cost in atomic writes.\n   - Tooling already in the tree: thiserror, miette, clap derive, serde/serde_json, tempfile, insta, yaml-rust2 (callisto-graph), proptest, rstest, schemars, serial_test, camino, globset/ignore, toml_edit, tracing; no tokio and no async anywhere; Windows is unsupported.\n   - Process learnings: semantic collisions between PRs that merge cleanly (error-code classification test, envelope test) turned main red twice; fail-fast test runs hide later failures; worktree guard rejects git-in-heredoc and complex commands; use scratch worktrees for real runs; verification script `verify.sh <dir>` runs pre-push, lint, doc-check, test, workflow-contracts by exit code outside the sandbox.\n   - Over-engineering patterns identified by me for the final critique: source-text scan tests, shell plus .txt pinning, a 21-invocation binary test duplicating what a trait guarantees, a hand-maintained 233-code string list, nine near-identical config error codes, Windows chunking for an unsupported platform, a process-global `note_once` static, a wall-clock perf assertion, narrator agents whose bodies I then rewrote, full verify on tiny follow-ups, agent briefs that never said \"prefer types\".\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `crates/callisto-cli/src/output.rs` (current uncommitted lean change on branch `fix/json-contract-complete`, #505): `write_json` became `pub(crate) fn write_document` with a doc comment (\"A report goes through emit_report... crate-private so a command cannot bypass it\") and a new `pub fn write_error_json(w, &serde_json::Value)`; callers updated in `lib.rs` (re-export `write_error_json`), `main.rs`, `commands/release.rs` (`inspect` uses `write_document`), `commands/version.rs` (test); `crates/callisto-cli/tests/json_contract_tests.rs` was deleted. `cargo build -p callisto-cli --all-targets` finished clean. Not yet committed or pushed.\n   - `crates/callisto-cli/src/error.rs`: `USAGE_CODES` (E195 E196 E214 E216 E217 E218 E224–E229 E231 E240 E241 E242 E270 E271 E274 E275 E276 E279 E282 E283 E298 E309 E324 E327 E329) and `USAGE_WHEN_FROM_FLAG_CODES = [\"E316\",\"E317\"]`; `CliError::is_usage_error()`; main.rs exits 2 for usage errors.\n   - `crates/callisto-cli/tests/usage_exit_codes_tests.rs`: classification test; `RUNTIME_CODES` string now includes E325 E326 E328 (and #506 adds E330–E339).\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/error.rs` and `config/resolve.rs`: E111 stays TOML syntax; E325 `UnknownField`; E326 `InvalidValue`; (#506) E330–E339; E254 retired.\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/changed.rs`: batched diff per tag commit and `PATHSPEC_BUDGET = 24_000` with `list_in_chunks` (flagged by me as speculative Windows handling to delete).\n   - `crates/callisto-model/src/vcs/access.rs` (#507): `first_parent_commits_by_path` with `\"log\",\"--no-show-signature\",\"--first-parent\",\"-m\",\"-z\",\"--name-only\",\"--relative\",\"--format=%x01%H\"`; `ReleaseOwners` in `release_decision.rs`.\n   - `crates/callisto-graph/src/note.rs` (#496): `note_once` with `OnceLock<Mutex<BTreeSet<String>>>` (flagged as un-Rusty global).\n   - #504 test-only files: `.github/tests/verify-action-inputs.sh`, `.github/tests/action-inputs/*.txt`, `justfile` line, `crates/callisto-cli/tests/tag_name_contract_tests.rs`, `config_deny_unknown_fields_guard_tests.rs` (source-text scan), an exit-code parse-error test.\n   - Docs/specs: `docs/stability.md`, `docs/errors.md`, `docs/specs/release.json`, `AGENTS.md` (changeset process line, PR template Changelog line), changesets under `.changeset/`.\n   - Memory: `/Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/feedback_rust_types_not_tests_lean.md` created, indexed at the top of `MEMORY.md`.\n   - Plugin repo `~/projects/wisp-plugins` (shared references `shared/references/{architecture,verification,hazards}/rust.md`): reviewed; the user is updating the plugins with another agent.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - Release preview/PR flow earlier: stale worktree conflicts, unsigned commits re-signed (user-approved earlier), spurious E169 fixed (#480).\n   - `git checkout -- .` after a real run wiped my uncommitted edits: recreated them, then committed immediately and ran real checks in scratch worktrees.\n   - Changeset id keys had no `.md`: tests passed with `a.md` but the real run showed no PR numbers; fixed by keying on the file stem.\n   - `git log` failed in a repo with no commits: treated \"does not have any commits yet\" as empty.\n   - Reused changeset name picked the oldest PR: switched to `entry().or_insert` (finding on #483).\n   - #484 (RELEASE_PR_TOKEN) failed the workflow secrets policy (\"secrets referenced outside execute\"); closed; my verify script had skipped `just workflow-contracts`, now included.\n   - Three agents' sandbox-only failures (network tests) re-verified outside the sandbox.\n   - `--diff-merges=first-parent` required git 2.31: replaced by `-m`.\n   - json-contract test would have contacted github.com: fixed with an unreachable local proxy (`http.proxy http://127.0.0.1:9`); `insteadOf` broke GitHub detection.\n   - Semantic collisions: #500+#502 made main red (classification test lacked E325–E328; envelope test expected exit 1 where E309 now exits 2): fixed in #503 and #501 (identical envelope-helper change; E327/E329 usage, E325/E326/E328 runtime); E325 collision resolved by renumbering the duplicate-package error to E329.\n   - E111 meaning move (my instruction) corrected so E111 stays \"not valid TOML\"; dotted pre tags (`beta.1`) kept working via a pre-specific validator.\n   - User feedback: unknown \"disinfectant in change 191\" (likely dictation slip) dropped; \"you're not thinking in the rust way and you're over-engineering way too much\".\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Merged this period: #480–#487, #490–#494, #496–#503 plus earlier ones (release 0.19.0 published, verified 5 assets and layered notes). Main CI green at `f06bb0fd5`.\n   - 1.0 stability audit findings all addressed: JSON envelopes, camelCase keys, exit 2, one condition per error code, unknown-key and tag validation, `add` duplicate rejection, `status` null fields, `--strict` help, `version_command` doc, bare-names stability wording.\n   - Performance: measured 1,800-package synthetic monorepo; fixed per-package diffs (#494) and two release-path hotspots (#507); remaining cost is macOS fsync in `version --emit-preview` (left alone, durability invariant).\n   - Release safety audit (#162): retry and hold design already built; remaining gap is a manual \"Ready for review\" override; recommended no new check, plus two small E165 help changes; recorded in trail description.\n   - Trails: 37 plus further closures; #198 open.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - \"Another… #77 we have some findings…\" through \"Go, start with markers\" (earlier segment, per prior summary).\n   - \"Keep going\"; \"Keep going on the rest of our work now please\"; \"Go, start with markers\" (twice).\n   - \"Keep going\"; \"What’s next? merged it\"; \"No idea what \"disinfectant in change 191\" means or where it came from\"; \"Maybe code review agent findings for that change?\"; \"Any open findings for callisto?\"; \"Any work we want to wrap up before the next release?\"; \"No blocking but anything else we feel should go in for a better experience for our users?\"; \"Are we missing trails for work?\"; \"Let's make the recommended work then? Didn't we just do some work that I don't see a trail for the marker work?\"; \"change #90 conflict finding\" (pasted finding on #482, fixed in #482); \"Start the next improvements please\"; \"Let's do A and B in parallel\"; \"Keep going\"; \"WTF did this fail? https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/pull/484 Is the workflow contract strick about wofklow files that a tiny change blows it? This is stupid and over-engineered no?\"; \"Was the PR change not needed?\"; \"What is the check guarding against? honestly? Does it really provide value to the amount of work this is?\"; \"And the goal wasn't to run the CI in main? WHy? Wasn't that valuable? Why did you propose it?\"; \"Don't worry, let's focus on value for callisto users\"; \"We have a code review finding for change #91 …\" (reused changeset name); \"Keep going on our features, fixes, and improvements\"; \"What does it mean that each change in the release PR now says which PR added it? Do we mean we add a link to the PR??\"; \"But do you feel like that changeset text communcates well…\"; \"It never mentions the link… Are you fucking dense?\"; \"I don't think we're being mindful and careful with our changeset text… WTF is this kind of embarassment?\"; \"Not only that but we want them to tell a useful set of information to the user right? Why do they care, what does it mean?\"; \"Should the changeset for emiting preview says something like… 'Adds commands to preview a release'…\"; \"How did you not understand my explanation… Stop rushing to changes and let's understand what the goal and outcomes are…\"; \"What else can we learn from other world class leaders in the industry about quality changelog entries?\"; \"We don't want a hard target. Being prescriptive is not great at generating value with LLMs… bullets and sub-bullets with sections…\"; \"Are we doing a terrible job in our plugins and need to make changes?\"; \"Why the fuck are not using the plugins agents???????????? What is the point?\"; \"Yes, lets update the plugins (we have a current checkout in ~/projects/wisp-plugins)…\"; \"Give me a prompt to give another agent…\"; \"Let's update our changesets based on these learnings while I work with the plugins\"; \"change #93 has a conflict\"; \"Are we not being mindful when creating branches?\"; \"Keep going on the work\"; \"What's next? How do we plan on making changesets and when?\"; \"Without extra work. Private mean no publishing…\"; \"What are the best practices in the industry? What would be the actual need to version and publish a root package?\"; \"ok, versioned but not published right?\"; \"We should make sure we distinguish the non-published versioned packages in Prs and the release right?\"; \"How do we plan on doing that?\"; \"Go with that plan, start with the did-you-mean PR. Do as much in parallel as possible. Do the work using agent teams and workflows with model and effort for quality and keeping in mind token cost.\"; \"Recommendations look good\"; \"What is your recommendation?\"; \"Keep going on feature work towards v1\"; \"Is this the best way to setup these tests?…\"; \"That is exactly what I'm thinking. Rust is structure for compiler checks… over-engineering way too much\"; \"What other places are we making similar mistakes…\"; \"What can we change about rust guidance to prefer types, traits, usage of common crates…\"; \"What kind of prompt and guidance would you give to another agent to look for similar patterns of issues?\" (last; with a strict text-only instruction).\n   - Also the earlier mid-session messages: \"Merged everything, you're slowing things down. Pickup up the pace\"; \"fix/cli-polish branch has conflicts and is out of date\"; \"Same with fix/input-validation\"; \"We have CI failures: …/pull/501\"; \"Let's fix it\"; \"Please resolve the conflicts: …trails/198/changes/111\"; \"Where is the error-envelope work?\"; \"The agent is still open\"; \"What’s next???\"; \"Let's include more of our improvements, stability, performance work into the next version\".\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Await the user's \"go\" on the lean cleanup: finish #505 lean (commit and push the `write_document` change, update its PR body/title, confirm no other users of `write_json`), trim #504 (delete shell script, `.txt` lists, `justfile` line, source-scan guard; optionally a small Rust insta test for Action inputs), delete the `PATHSPEC_BUDGET` chunking (#494) and the wall-clock test (#507), consider type-based exit-code classification and consolidating E330–E338, and note_once without a global.\n   - Await go on the Rust guidance: add a ~10-line \"Rust way\" section to Callisto AGENTS.md, a few clippy lints (`wildcard_enum_match_arm`, `disallowed_macros` for `json!`, `print_stdout`/`print_stderr`), and give the user plugin text (new `shared/references/authoring/rust.md`, a row in `verification/rust.md`, two failure modes in `smith:implementer`).\n   - Await user review/merge of #495, #504, #505, #506, #507; then rebase/resolve as needed and read #493 as a user before merging; confirm the 0.20.0 changelog.\n   - Decisions pending: two small E165 help changes (yes/no); macOS fsync look; locked agent worktrees (`error-envelope`, `input-validation`, `note-once`) need `/agents` or \"force it\"; `short_sha` was fixed (camelCase) in #498.\n   - Prepare, when asked, the reviewer-agent prompt for finding over-engineering patterns (the final question).\n\n8. Current Work:\n   Immediately before this summary request the user asked: \"What kind of prompt and guidance would you give to another agent to look for similar patterns of issues?\" with a text-only instruction. Prior to that I had just answered the guidance question (AGENTS.md section, clippy lints, plugin reference) and was mid-way through making #505 lean in worktree `agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4` (branch `fix/json-contract-complete`): edits applied and build verified, nothing committed. The reviewer-agent brief I intended to give: a read-only audit agent (bug-hunter or Explore, Sonnet) told to find, with file:line evidence, (1) tests that duplicate what a type or trait bound guarantees; (2) source-text scans, shell scripts and text lists where Rust or insta would do; (3) hand-maintained string lists mirroring code (exit-code/error-code lists, command allow-lists) and stringly-typed matching on error codes; (4) wall-clock assertions instead of call counting; (5) code for unsupported platforms or hypothetical limits (Windows is unsupported); (6) process-global state where a returned structure would do; (7) public surface growth that freezes at 1.0 (error codes, JSON keys, flags) that could be consolidated; (8) duplicated near-identical tests collapsible with rstest/table tests; (9) crate misuse or reinvention when a crate already in the tree fits (thiserror/miette/clap/serde/insta/proptest/tempfile; no tokio); (10) agent/process waste (full verify on tiny edits, bodies rewritten after generation, briefs missing \"prefer types, delete before adding\"). Output: a ranked table (finding, evidence, simplest replacement, deletion candidates, risk), propose deletions before additions, no edits, under ~600 words.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Await the user's \"go\". If they approve, do the lean cleanup myself first (commit and push the lean #505, then trim #504, drop `PATHSPEC_BUDGET` and the wall-clock test), and send the AGENTS.md and clippy PR plus the plugin text. Verbatim anchor from the last exchange: \"What kind of prompt and guidance would you give to another agent to look for similar patterns of issues?\" — the immediate step is to deliver the reviewer-agent prompt described above as the answer, not to start new work unprompted.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto--claude-worktrees-agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4/6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"c687eb3b-190c-4f1c-827b-3315135a801a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:54:00.677Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":201,"turnIndex":259},"turnOrigin":"human","summarizedTurnPromptUuids":["ac322499-59ab-4b44-9868-61dfee48a2ac"],"session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"c687eb3b-190c-4f1c-827b-3315135a801a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/feedback_rust_types_not_tests_lean.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/feedback_rust_types_not_tests_lean.md","content":"---\nname: rust-types-not-tests-lean\ndescription: \"Prefer compiler-enforced structure over tests, shell, text scans and string lists; delete before adding; no speculative generality. User called out over-engineering in the 0.20 stability batch.\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8\n  modified: 2026-11-11T17:48:56.843Z\n---\n\nIn Rust, enforce invariants with types (a trait bound, a private constructor, an exhaustive `match`) so the compiler checks them. A test is the fallback for what types cannot see, and it should be small.\n\n**Why:** In the 0.20 stability batch (2026-11-11) I let implementer agents add shell scripts plus text files to pin Action inputs, a source-text scan for `deny_unknown_fields`, a 21-invocation binary test for `schemaVersion` that the `Report` trait already guaranteed, a 233-code string list to classify exit codes, a Windows-length pathspec budget for an unsupported platform, and 15 new error codes (each a permanent frozen API). The user said \"you're not thinking in the rust way and you're over-engineering way too much\". The repo had already deleted a source-grepping test (#488).\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Before adding a test, ask whether a type or a private function makes the bug unrepresentable; do that instead. Reuse what is already in the tree (`insta`, `yaml-rust2`, `emit_report`/`Report`) rather than shell or text files.\n- No source-text scanning tests, no hand-maintained lists of strings that mirror code, no wall-clock assertions (count calls instead).\n- No code for unsupported platforms or hypothetical limits (docs/stability.md says Windows is unsupported).\n- Every new public surface (an error code, a JSON key, a flag) is permanent after 1.0: prefer fewer, broader ones.\n- Put \"prefer types, delete before adding, no speculative generality\" in every implementer brief, and review agent output for these before pushing.\n\nRelated: [[feedback_no_overengineering_confirm_intent]], [[feedback_measure_value_before_building]].\n","numLines":23,"startLine":1,"totalLines":23}},"displayPath":"../../../../../.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/feedback_rust_types_not_tests_lean.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0f1c4999-63cc-47ba-84a0-130ca7f26ec9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:54:00.684Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/feedback_rust_types_not_tests_lean.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t---\n2\tname: rust-types-not-tests-lean\n3\tdescription: \"Prefer compiler-enforced structure over tests, shell, text scans and string lists; delete before adding; no speculative generality. User called out over-engineering in the 0.20 stability batch.\"\n4\tmetadata:\n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: feedback\n7\t  originSessionId: 6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8\n8\t  modified: 2026-11-11T17:48:56.843Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tIn Rust, enforce invariants with types (a trait bound, a private constructor, an exhaustive `match`) so the compiler checks them. A test is the fallback for what types cannot see, and it should be small.\n12\t\n13\t**Why:** In the 0.20 stability batch (2026-11-11) I let implementer agents add shell scripts plus text files to pin Action inputs, a source-text scan for `deny_unknown_fields`, a 21-invocation binary test for `schemaVersion` that the `Report` trait already guaranteed, a 233-code string list to classify exit codes, a Windows-length pathspec budget for an unsupported platform, and 15 new error codes (each a permanent frozen API). The user said \"you're not thinking in the rust way and you're over-engineering way too much\". The repo had already deleted a source-grepping test (#488).\n14\t\n15\t**How to apply:**\n16\t- Before adding a test, ask whether a type or a private function makes the bug unrepresentable; do that instead. Reuse what is already in the tree (`insta`, `yaml-rust2`, `emit_report`/`Report`) rather than shell or text files.\n17\t- No source-text scanning tests, no hand-maintained lists of strings that mirror code, no wall-clock assertions (count calls instead).\n18\t- No code for unsupported platforms or hypothetical limits (docs/stability.md says Windows is unsupported).\n19\t- Every new public surface (an error code, a JSON key, a flag) is permanent after 1.0: prefer fewer, broader ones.\n20\t- Put \"prefer types, delete before adding, no speculative generality\" in every implementer brief, and review agent output for these before pushing.\n21\t\n22\tRelated: [[feedback_no_overengineering_confirm_intent]], [[feedback_measure_value_before_building]].\n23\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"0f1c4999-63cc-47ba-84a0-130ca7f26ec9","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/close_write_json.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/close_write_json.py","content":"root = \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4/crates/callisto-cli/\"\n\n\ndef sub(path, old, new, count=1):\n    s = open(root + path).read()\n    assert s.count(old) == count, (path, s.count(old), old[:60])\n    open(root + path, \"w\").write(s.replace(old, new))\n\n\nsub(\n    \"src/output.rs\",\n    \"\"\"pub fn write_json<W: io::Write, S: Serialize + ?Sized>(w: &mut W, val: &S) -> io::Result<()> {\n    let text = serde_json::to_string_pretty(val)?;\n    writeln!(w, \"{text}\")\n}\n\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"/// Writes a JSON document that is not a report, such as the intent `release inspect` echoes, which carries its own\n/// version. A report goes through [`emit_report`], the only way to get the envelope and `schemaVersion`; this is\n/// crate-private so a command cannot bypass it.\npub(crate) fn write_document<W: io::Write, S: Serialize + ?Sized>(w: &mut W, val: &S) -> io::Result<()> {\n    let text = serde_json::to_string_pretty(val)?;\n    writeln!(w, \"{text}\")\n}\n\n/// Writes the JSON error envelope that `format_error_json` builds.\npub fn write_error_json<W: io::Write>(w: &mut W, envelope: &serde_json::Value) -> io::Result<()> {\n    write_document(w, envelope)\n}\n\"\"\",\n)\nsub(\"src/output.rs\", \"write_json(&mut buf, &FakeReport { value: 42 })\", \"write_document(&mut buf, &FakeReport { value: 42 })\")\nsub(\"src/lib.rs\", \"pub use output::{log_line, write_json};\", \"pub use output::{log_line, write_error_json};\")\nsub(\"src/main.rs\", \"callisto_cli::output::write_json(&mut std::io::stderr(), &report)\", \"callisto_cli::output::write_error_json(&mut std::io::stderr(), &report)\")\nsub(\"src/commands/release.rs\", \"use crate::output::{emit_line, emit_report, log_line, write_json};\", \"use crate::output::{emit_line, emit_report, log_line, write_document};\")\nsub(\"src/commands/release.rs\", \"OutputFormat::Json => write_json(&mut std::io::stdout(), &value)?\", \"OutputFormat::Json => write_document(&mut std::io::stdout(), &value)?\")\nsub(\"src/commands/version.rs\", \"the JSON output of `write_json(&report)`\", \"the JSON output of `write_document(&report)`\")\nsub(\"src/commands/version.rs\", \"crate::output::write_json(&mut json_out, &report)\", \"crate::output::write_document(&mut json_out, &report)\")\nprint(\"ok\")\n","numLines":39,"startLine":1,"totalLines":39}},"displayPath":"../../../../../.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/close_write_json.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0772124f-a390-4376-bbdd-c4ec1cbf7f4d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:54:00.684Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/close_write_json.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\troot = \"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4/crates/callisto-cli/\"\n2\t\n3\t\n4\tdef sub(path, old, new, count=1):\n5\t    s = open(root + path).read()\n6\t    assert s.count(old) == count, (path, s.count(old), old[:60])\n7\t    open(root + path, \"w\").write(s.replace(old, new))\n8\t\n9\t\n10\tsub(\n11\t    \"src/output.rs\",\n12\t    \"\"\"pub fn write_json<W: io::Write, S: Serialize + ?Sized>(w: &mut W, val: &S) -> io::Result<()> {\n13\t    let text = serde_json::to_string_pretty(val)?;\n14\t    writeln!(w, \"{text}\")\n15\t}\n16\t\"\"\",\n17\t    \"\"\"/// Writes a JSON document that is not a report, such as the intent `release inspect` echoes, which carries its own\n18\t/// version. A report goes through [`emit_report`], the only way to get the envelope and `schemaVersion`; this is\n19\t/// crate-private so a command cannot bypass it.\n20\tpub(crate) fn write_document<W: io::Write, S: Serialize + ?Sized>(w: &mut W, val: &S) -> io::Result<()> {\n21\t    let text = serde_json::to_string_pretty(val)?;\n22\t    writeln!(w, \"{text}\")\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t/// Writes the JSON error envelope that `format_error_json` builds.\n26\tpub fn write_error_json<W: io::Write>(w: &mut W, envelope: &serde_json::Value) -> io::Result<()> {\n27\t    write_document(w, envelope)\n28\t}\n29\t\"\"\",\n30\t)\n31\tsub(\"src/output.rs\", \"write_json(&mut buf, &FakeReport { value: 42 })\", \"write_document(&mut buf, &FakeReport { value: 42 })\")\n32\tsub(\"src/lib.rs\", \"pub use output::{log_line, write_json};\", \"pub use output::{log_line, write_error_json};\")\n33\tsub(\"src/main.rs\", \"callisto_cli::output::write_json(&mut std::io::stderr(), &report)\", \"callisto_cli::output::write_error_json(&mut std::io::stderr(), &report)\")\n34\tsub(\"src/commands/release.rs\", \"use crate::output::{emit_line, emit_report, log_line, write_json};\", \"use crate::output::{emit_line, emit_report, log_line, write_document};\")\n35\tsub(\"src/commands/release.rs\", \"OutputFormat::Json => write_json(&mut std::io::stdout(), &value)?\", \"OutputFormat::Json => write_document(&mut std::io::stdout(), &value)?\")\n36\tsub(\"src/commands/version.rs\", \"the JSON output of `write_json(&report)`\", \"the JSON output of `write_document(&report)`\")\n37\tsub(\"src/commands/version.rs\", \"crate::output::write_json(&mut json_out, &report)\", \"crate::output::write_document(&mut json_out, &report)\")\n38\tprint(\"ok\")\n39\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"0772124f-a390-4376-bbdd-c4ec1cbf7f4d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/pr507_final.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/pr507_final.md","content":"## Summary\n\nPlanning a release in a very large monorepo scaled with the number of releasing packages, so `callisto release` and the release PR body slowed sharply as more packages shipped together. Two causes, found by the same benchmark on one planning path:\n\n- The preflight that checks a package has not moved past its version commit ran one `git log` and one `git cat-file --batch` per package. It now runs one `git log` for all manifests (`first_parent_commits_by_path` in `callisto-model`, using `--first-parent -m` and literal pathspecs) plus one batched read. A package whose newest candidate is not the version-setting commit falls back to its own read, so results are unchanged.\n- `workspace_package` rescanned every package and recomputed identities on each call. It is replaced by a map built once (`ReleaseOwners`), every call site is migrated, and per-selection linear scans are now set lookups.\n\nMeasured on a synthetic monorepo (1,800 packages, 1,500 depending on one core package, 1,000 pending changesets, 20,000 tags): `release --dry-run` 13.9 s to 1.85 s and 3,012 git processes to 14 (debug), 8.10 s to 1.27 s (release build). With 20,000 commits it goes from 7.95 s to 1.63 s, and `compose-pr-body` from 12.1 s to 1.45 s (debug) and 1.90 s to 0.76 s (release). `status`, `version --dry-run` and the other dimensions benchmarked flat or linear and are untouched.\n\n## Verify\n\nOld and new output are byte-identical for `release --dry-run` text and JSON and for `compose-pr-body` on both large cases.\n\nA counting-runner test pins one `git log` and a bounded `cat-file` count at 3 and at 12 packages, and two mutations fail it. A 3,000-package test fails if the index is rebuilt per lookup (18 s against 0.04 s); it is wall-clock based with about a 250x margin, so only a very loaded CI machine risks a flake. The existing version-commit tests, including a 105-edit and merge-commit cases, pass.\n\n`just pre-push`, `just lint`, `just doc-check`, `just test` and `just workflow-contracts` exit 0. CI has not run.\n\nNot changed: `version --emit-preview` takes 33 to 45 s when thousands of packages are bumped, about 75% of it macOS `F_FULLFSYNC` from the per-file and per-directory fsync. That is the durability invariant in AGENTS.md, so it is left alone.\n","numLines":19,"startLine":1,"totalLines":19}},"displayPath":"../../../../../.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/pr507_final.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d77d0451-3890-454e-b500-8fdea13cd61b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:54:00.684Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/pr507_final.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t## Summary\n2\t\n3\tPlanning a release in a very large monorepo scaled with the number of releasing packages, so `callisto release` and the release PR body slowed sharply as more packages shipped together. Two causes, found by the same benchmark on one planning path:\n4\t\n5\t- The preflight that checks a package has not moved past its version commit ran one `git log` and one `git cat-file --batch` per package. It now runs one `git log` for all manifests (`first_parent_commits_by_path` in `callisto-model`, using `--first-parent -m` and literal pathspecs) plus one batched read. A package whose newest candidate is not the version-setting commit falls back to its own read, so results are unchanged.\n6\t- `workspace_package` rescanned every package and recomputed identities on each call. It is replaced by a map built once (`ReleaseOwners`), every call site is migrated, and per-selection linear scans are now set lookups.\n7\t\n8\tMeasured on a synthetic monorepo (1,800 packages, 1,500 depending on one core package, 1,000 pending changesets, 20,000 tags): `release --dry-run` 13.9 s to 1.85 s and 3,012 git processes to 14 (debug), 8.10 s to 1.27 s (release build). With 20,000 commits it goes from 7.95 s to 1.63 s, and `compose-pr-body` from 12.1 s to 1.45 s (debug) and 1.90 s to 0.76 s (release). `status`, `version --dry-run` and the other dimensions benchmarked flat or linear and are untouched.\n9\t\n10\t## Verify\n11\t\n12\tOld and new output are byte-identical for `release --dry-run` text and JSON and for `compose-pr-body` on both large cases.\n13\t\n14\tA counting-runner test pins one `git log` and a bounded `cat-file` count at 3 and at 12 packages, and two mutations fail it. A 3,000-package test fails if the index is rebuilt per lookup (18 s against 0.04 s); it is wall-clock based with about a 250x margin, so only a very loaded CI machine risks a flake. The existing version-commit tests, including a 105-edit and merge-commit cases, pass.\n15\t\n16\t`just pre-push`, `just lint`, `just doc-check`, `just test` and `just workflow-contracts` exit 0. CI has not run.\n17\t\n18\tNot changed: `version --emit-preview` takes 33 to 45 s when thousands of packages are bumped, about 75% of it macOS `F_FULLFSYNC` from the per-file and per-directory fsync. That is the durability invariant in AGENTS.md, so it is left alone.\n19\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"d77d0451-3890-454e-b500-8fdea13cd61b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/overlap6.sh","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/overlap6.sh","content":"cd /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-a3658a1f85bca2d75 || exit 1\ngit fetch -q origin\nfor other in test/stability-contracts fix/json-contract-complete fix/error-code-reach; do\n  if git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only origin/perf/scale-hotspots \"origin/$other\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo \"clean    perf + $other\"; else echo \"CONFLICT perf + $other\"; git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only origin/perf/scale-hotspots \"origin/$other\" 2>&1 | grep \"^CONFLICT\" | head -3; fi\ndone\n","numLines":6,"startLine":1,"totalLines":6}},"displayPath":"../../../../../.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/overlap6.sh"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d3741fed-3b68-4238-a6c8-851ac84cf57e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:54:00.684Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/6cc3e47b/tmp/overlap6.sh\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\tcd /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-a3658a1f85bca2d75 || exit 1\n2\tgit fetch -q origin\n3\tfor other in test/stability-contracts fix/json-contract-complete fix/error-code-reach; do\n4\t  if git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only origin/perf/scale-hotspots \"origin/$other\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo \"clean    perf + $other\"; else echo \"CONFLICT perf + $other\"; git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only origin/perf/scale-hotspots \"origin/$other\" 2>&1 | grep \"^CONFLICT\" | head -3; fi\n5\tdone\n6\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"d3741fed-3b68-4238-a6c8-851ac84cf57e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-a3658a1f85bca2d75/.changeset/faster-release-planning.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-a3658a1f85bca2d75/.changeset/faster-release-planning.md","content":"---\ncallisto-cli: patch\n---\n\n`callisto release` and the release PR are much faster in very large monorepos.\n\n- With about 1,500 of 1,800 packages releasing at once, `release --dry-run` starts 14 git processes instead of about 3,000: 8.1 s to 1.3 s in a release build, and 7.9 s to 1.6 s on a 20,000-commit history.\n- `compose-pr-body --publish-plan` no longer slows down quadratically as more packages release at once: 12 s to 1.5 s in a debug build at 1,800 packages.\n- The output is unchanged.\n","numLines":10,"startLine":1,"totalLines":10}},"displayPath":"../agent-a3658a1f85bca2d75/.changeset/faster-release-planning.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"cb6dbfd6-cc3c-4171-aa02-f1fb647d2334","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:54:00.684Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-a3658a1f85bca2d75/.changeset/faster-release-planning.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t---\n2\tcallisto-cli: patch\n3\t---\n4\t\n5\t`callisto release` and the release PR are much faster in very large monorepos.\n6\t\n7\t- With about 1,500 of 1,800 packages releasing at once, `release --dry-run` starts 14 git processes instead of about 3,000: 8.1 s to 1.3 s in a release build, and 7.9 s to 1.6 s on a 20,000-commit history.\n8\t- `compose-pr-body --publish-plan` no longer slows down quadratically as more packages release at once: 12 s to 1.5 s in a debug build at 1,800 packages.\n9\t- The output is unchanged.\n10\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-aafeb13134f6a0ae4","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"fix/json-contract-complete","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
