{"parentUuid":"0f14f94e-45f3-4a48-9179-9b76d9802a85","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"474e67de-7144-489a-a3b6-bb9daa7981bc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ZgxvrSTaDGNsFTaNat5jA","type":"tool_result","content":"# Principles\n\nTwo questions come up over and over: **should this go in michi?** and **how should this work get done?** This doc answers both, so nobody — human or agent — has to re-derive the reasoning from scratch each time. It's the\n\n---\n\n## What belongs in michi\n\nA primitive earns a place here only if it clears all five gates below. These are yes/no checks with evidence, not vibes — if you can't point to the evidence, it doesn't clear the gate yet.\n\n### 1. Rendering, not domain logic\n\nIt turns already-decided data into agent-readable strings/types. It doesn't decide _what_ data survives.\n\n- One established exception: char/byte-level truncation (`truncate`, `ToonOptions::max_cell_len`) — treated as rendering safety, not business logic.\n- Rendering makes output syntactically safe, not trustworthy: it keeps one value from creating extra lines, fields or cells, and it does not judge what a value says. See the [trust model](docs/spec/02-toon-format.md#trus\n- **The ceiling:** if a tool's real need is more sophisticated than a generic primitive can serve — a custom ranking algorithm, a heuristic tuned to one tool's data shape — that's a sign the primitive doesn't belong in m\n\n### 2. Small default dependency set\n\nThe default build depends only on `thiserror` and `compact_str`. Anything else is opt-in behind a Cargo feature.\n\n- Adding a mandatory dependency needs a recorded decision in `docs/spec/06-decisions.md`. Prefer caller-supplied injection (a closure, a trait) first.\n\n### 3. Genuinely cross-tool — verified, not assumed\n\nEarns inclusion when independent, real tools would otherwise hand-roll the identical pattern.\n\n- \"Independent\" and \"real\" are load-bearing. One tool using a pattern heavily is one data point, not proof.\n- **What clearing this bar looks like:** a survey across several real agent-facing dev tools (a code-search engine, a CLI proxy, a context-compression tool, a code-graph server) found `help[]`-shaped \"note for the agent\"\n- A pattern seen in only one tool — however heavily repeated inside that tool — isn't there yet. Watch for a second independent confirmation before building it.\n\n### 4. Doesn't duplicate an existing primitive\n\nCheck `hints` / `recovery` / `idempotency` / `status` / `error` / `mcp` first. Verify the distinction concretely — don't assume novelty from a vibe.\n\n- \"This looks similar to X\" means go read X's source, not assume it's covered.\n- Example: a proposed \"diagnostics\" primitive looked adjacent to `idempotency::PartialSuccess` — until actually reading `PartialSuccess`'s doc comment showed it's scoped to multi-step _mutating_ operations, not read-path\n\n### 5. Deviations get tracked, not silently absorbed\n\nWhere the final shape diverges from the original ask or an earlier draft, it goes in a Design Decisions table. Spec and code should never quietly drift apart.\n\n---\n\n## Split the decision from the signal\n\nGate 1's ceiling (\"stay generic\" vs. \"real tools need real sophistication\") isn't a tension to compromise on — it's usually an architecture problem. Separate the _domain-specific decision_ from the _universal signal_.\n\n**Worked example: truncation.** Deciding _which_ items survive a budget is tool-specific — personalized-PageRank over a call graph, a statistical outlier-preserving sampler, a simple first-N cutoff. None of that belongs \n\nThat split shipped as `michi_core::truncation` (API in [`docs/spec/03-rust-api.md`](docs/spec/03-rust-api.md#truncation--what-a-list-cap-cut)): `TruncationOutcome<T>` holds what was kept and the total before the cut, `hi\n\nA caller with a genuinely novel heuristic never has to fit their algorithm into michi's shape — they wrap whatever they decided with `TruncationOutcome::new` and get the same hint/report format everyone else gets. Michi \n\n**When a new primitive seems too domain-specific to generalize, ask whether it splits this way first.** Often only half of it actually is.\n\n---\n\n## How work gets done\n----\ncrates/michi-core/src/redact.rs:16:pub const REDACTED: &str = \"[REDACTED]\";\ncrates/michi-core/src/redact.rs:30:pub fn redact_secrets(text: &str, secrets: &[&str]) -> String {\ncrates/michi-core/src/redact.rs:66:pub fn redact_url_userinfo(text: &str) -> String {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:19:pub struct RetryConfig {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:35:pub enum RetryConfigError {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:135:pub fn next_retry_delay(\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:173:pub fn parse_retry_after(header_value: &str) -> Option<Duration> {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:179:pub fn parse_retry_after_at(header_value: &str, now: std::time::SystemTime) -> Option<Duration> {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:358:pub fn is_retryable_status(status: u16) -> bool {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:364:pub struct IdempotencyKey(pub String);\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:407:pub enum AlreadyDone {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:419:pub fn already_done(stored: Option<String>) -> AlreadyDone {\ncrates/michi-core/src/resilience.rs:430:pub fn render_already_done(operation: &str, summary: &str, hints: &[String]) -> String {\ncrates/michi-core/src/error.rs:9:pub enum ErrorClass {\ncrates/michi-core/src/error.rs:30:pub const MAY_HAVE_TAKEN_EFFECT_HINT: &str =\ncrates/michi-core/src/error.rs:38:pub struct Sensitive<T>(pub T);\ncrates/michi-core/src/error.rs:60:pub enum ErrorCode {\ncrates/michi-core/src/error.rs:145:pub struct DomainError {\ncrates/michi-core/src/error.rs:554:pub enum Error {\ncrates/michi-core/src/recovery.rs:17:pub struct RecoveryHint {\ncrates/michi-core/src/recovery.rs:189:pub fn render_recovery(hints: &[RecoveryHint]) -> String {\ncrates/mich