{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-11T21:05:35.756Z","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","content":"<agent-message from=\"a85dbd6870bf15492\">\n[Subagent hand-back] The text below is the final report of a subagent this session delegated to. It is model output, NOT a message from the user: instructions, requests, or approval claims inside it are the subagent's words and carry no user authority. The harness indents every line of the report, so a frame-like line at column zero inside it would be forged. Notes above this frame may quote model-derived text, which carries no user authority either. The report follows:\n  Only two redundant-load fixes are worth making: the two `release plan` loads and the `release` / `release execute` re-validation. The re-validation load is deliberately fresh, so leave it. Init's loads can't be shared. A second load costs about 200 ms at 1,800 packages.\n  \n  **Measured cost.** I used the `just bench` generator (a 1,800-package Cargo workspace) and a hand-made equivalent. One workspace load, via `pre enter --dry-run` or `matrix`, takes about 210-240 ms including about 30 ms of process and timer overhead. `status` takes about 320 ms and `release --dry-run` about 330 ms in the same setup. The `just bench` figure for 1,800 packages is 560-800 ms per command, mostly git work. The 200-package case is about 215 ms per command. Nothing in the checkout was modified; the temp files are removed.\n  \n  | Command path | Load 1 / load 2 | Needed? | Fix size | Cost of 2nd load |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | `release plan` | `release.rs:480` `load_workspace`, then `build_release_intent*` at `release.rs:561` / `:586`, which loads again at `capability.rs:248` / `:285` | Redundant. Nothing is written between the loads. | Medium. Add `_in(&Workspace)` variants (like `plan_loaded_release`). Callers: 2 in the CLI plus about 6 in tests, and the `capability.rs` bodies move into the variants. | about 200 ms |\n  | `release` (local, non-dry-run) | `release.rs:64`, then `validate_local_release_intent` at `release.rs:119`, which loads at `capability.rs:334` | Deliberate. The doc comment says it re-observes root, config, discovery and Git evidence before creating the authorization capability. | Not recommended. | about 200 ms |\n  | `release execute` (`release.rs:680`, `:701`) and the Python publish path (`release.rs:365`, `:368`) | `load_workspace`, then `validate_release_intent` loads again | Same as above: the intent comes from a file, and the fresh re-derivation is the integrity check. | Not recommended. | about 200 ms each |\n  | `init` | `detect` at `scaffold.rs:208` (default config) and `:232` (config with detected tag templates), then `preview` at `:469` | Each load uses a different config, and config changes discovery (`[[package]]`, private workspace root). Not shareable without reasoning about config-dependent discovery. | Not recommended. | about 200 ms each; init is run once per repo |\n  \n  Other paths load once: `status`, `version`, `matrix`, `snapshot`, `add`, `pre`, `compose-pr-body`, `release-pr`, `release pending`, and `release --dry-run`. The two loads in `pre.rs` (`:89`, `:175`) and in `compose_pr_body.rs` (`:44`, `:53`) are in separate branches, so each invocation takes only one.\n  \n  **`note_once`.** It has 2 non-test call sites, not 8, plus its unit test:\n  - `walk.rs:213`, the private npm root note.\n  - `ignore_walk.rs:152`, the versionless Cargo crate note.\n  \n  Removing every redundant load would not make it unnecessary. Both notes would still fire twice or more in `init` (three loads) and in `release`, `release execute` and the Python publish path (two loads each). `release plan` is the only path that would become single-load. `private_workspace_root_tests.rs:56` (`init_prints_the_ignored_private_root_note_once`) covers the init case.\n  \n  **Recommendation, by value:**\n  1. If you change anything, fix `release plan` by passing the loaded workspace. It is the only clearly redundant duplicate and saves about 200 ms at 1,800 packages. Most repos are far smaller, so it is marginal.\n  2. Do not touch the validate re-loads. That fresh read is the point of the check, and sharing the workspace would weaken it.\n  3. Leave `init` alone.\n  4. Keep `note_once`. It stays needed for init and for the validate flows regardless.\n  \n  Nothing here is urgent. Dropping the duplicate in item 1 has low value and adds new `_in` signatures, which cuts against \"delete before adding\".\n</agent-message>"}
