r), so the major-requires-breaking rule is not triggered. Drop that field if the repo convention reserves it for major bumps. Reasoning notes that the diff body was only checked via --stat; the code list comes from your facts.","handback":true,"handbackReportText":"Decision: fold into the existing entry `.changeset/usage-errors-exit-2.md` (same outcome: bad flag now exits 2). Front matter stays `callisto-cli: minor`. No new file.\n\nFile: /.changeset/usage-errors-exit-2.md (full updated contents)\n\n```\n---\ncallisto-cli: minor\n---\n\nUsage errors now exit 2, as the stability promise says, so a script can tell a mistake in the command from a failure in the workspace.\n\n- A bad or missing flag value that Callisto checks after parsing now exits 2, like a clap-level mistake always did. Examples: `add --package a:patch`, `add --package cargo/a:bogus`, `add` without `--summary`, `init` without `--yes` and without a terminal, `snapshot --tag` with a space, and `--dry-run` on commands that need an output file.\n- Also exit 2: an unknown package passed to `--package` on `add`, `matrix` or `release` (E102), an invalid `init --forge-repository` (E187) or `--product-package` (E189), a `release pending --from` that is not a release commit (E328), a repeated `--package` in a release selection (E169), an invalid `--decision`, `--preview` or `--snapshot` value (E281), `release execute` flags that contradict the intent (E232, E233), and a `release plan --artifact-repository` that does not match the config (E230).\n- The rule: exit 2 when a flag value or combination is rejected, exit 1 when a valid flag meets state that cannot support it. Failures about your workspace, config, git, registries or release intent (for example E188, E299, E219, E308) still exit 1, and so does the same value read from `callisto.toml`, a changeset or `pre.json`.\n- The JSON error envelope is unchanged.\n\nAction: a script that checks for exit code 1 after a bad argument must also accept 2. Checking for any non-zero code is unaffected.\n```\n\nchangeset@3 JSON:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"summary\": \"Usage errors now exit 2, as the stability promise says, so a script can tell a mistake in the command from a failure in the workspace.\\n\\n- A bad or missing flag value that Callisto checks after parsing now exits 2, like a clap-level mistake always did. Examples: `add --package a:patch`, `add --package cargo/a:bogus`, `add` without `--summary`, `init` without `--yes` and without a terminal, `snapshot --tag` with a space, and `--dry-run` on commands that need an output file.\\n- Also exit 2: an unknown package passed to `--package` on `add`, `matrix` or `release` (E102), an invalid `init --forge-repository` (E187) or `--product-package` (E189), a `release pending --from` that is not a release commit (E328), a repeated `--package` in a release selection (E169), an invalid `--decision`, `--preview` or `--snapshot` value (E281), `release execute` flags that contradict the intent (E232, E233), and a `release plan --artifact-repository` that does not match the config (E230).\\n- The rule: exit 2 when a flag value or combination is rejected, exit 1 when a valid flag meets state that cannot support it. Failures about your workspace, config, git, registries or release intent (for example E188, E299, E219, E308) still exit 1, and so does the same value read from `callisto.toml`, a changeset or `pre.json`.\\n- The JSON error envelope is unchanged.\\n\\nAction: a script that checks for exit code 1 after a bad argument must also accept 2. Checking for any non-zero code is unaffected.\",\n    \"disposition\": \"update-existing\",\n    \"updates\": \".changeset/usage-errors-exit-2.md\",\n    \"consumer_impact\": \"behavior-change\",\n    \"semver_impact\": \"minor\",\n    \"files_changed\": [\n      \"crates/callisto-cli/src/commands/release.rs\",\n      \"crates/callisto-cli/src/error.rs\",\n      \"crates/callisto-graph/src/error.rs\",\n      \"docs/specs/cli.json\",\n      \"docs/specs/matrix.json\",\n      \"docs/specs/release.json\",\n      \"docs/stability.md\"\n    ],\n    \"tests_added\": [\"crates/callisto-cli/tests/usage_exit_codes_tests.rs\"],\n    \"acceptance_criteria_met\": [\"unlinked\"],\n    \"breaking_changes\": [\n      \"Flag-caused failures listed above (E102, E187, E189, E328, E169, E281, E232, E233, E230): old behavior exit 1 -> new behavior exit 2 -> a script that tests for exactly 1 after a bad argument must also accept 2; checking for any non-zero code is unaffected.\"\n    ],\n    \"commits\": [\"80caa64ce\"],\n    \"reasoning\": \"Same user-visible outcome as the unreleased usage-errors-exit-2 entry (bad argument exits 2); extends its coverage, so update-existing rather than a second near-duplicate entry. Semver: exit code of failure paths is a behavior change that the existing entry already classed as minor (pre-1.0 CLI); kept minor, not major, to stay consistent with that entry and because the entry is unreleased. No spec linked. Tests file is usage_exit_codes_tests.rs; the test file was also in the diff so tests_added lists it though it modifies an existing file. Did not inspect the diff body beyond --stat; error-code lists come from the caller's stated facts.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nNote: breaking_changes is populated because consumer behavior changes, but semver stays minor (not major), so the major-requires-breaking rule is not triggered. Drop that field if the repo convention reserves it for major bumps. Reasoning notes that the diff body was only checked via --stat; the code list comes from your facts."},"timestamp":"2026-11-11T23:09:28.974Z","isMeta":true,"reminderId":"ddba3c597d6321b2"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"49ffd1e4-3bd5-461b-8fec-963be1930fc7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T23:09:28.974Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder id=\"ddba3c597d6321b2\">\nAnother Claude session sent a message while you were working:\n<agent-message from=\"af9c27de6f014f85e\">\n[Subagent hand-back] The text below is the final re