{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","prNumber":529,"prUrl":"https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/pull/529","prRepository":"orin-dx/callisto","timestamp":"2026-11-12T01:18:14.566Z"}
{"parentUuid":"297fc25d-b06f-4136-beea-8ea454d50696","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e78817cf-c9bb-40a3-ad6b-22158834e6a5","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F7q3aFrM1Cua3UAhQXWRVA","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/feedback_rust_types_not_tests_lean.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"4b604f44-73d4-4bad-a0ac-bfc047cb571c","timestamp":"2026-11-12T01:18:14.489Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/.claude/projects/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/memory/feedback_rust_types_not_tests_lean.md","oldString":"Related: [[feedback_no_overengineering_confirm_intent]]","newString":"**Again (2026-11-12):** I launched PR #529, 8 insta snapshots (2,800 lines, 95 KB) of every report schema, because an audit ranked \"no golden for report shapes\" as 1.0 blocker #1; the user called it over-engineering and I closed it. An audit's ranked list is a lead, not a mandate: before launching a build agent, ask what concrete failure it prevents, who is hurt (the owner is the only user), and what it costs on every later change (it already conflicted with #526). Prefer a compact check or nothing.\n\nRelated: [[feedback_no_overengineering_confirm_intent]]","originalFile":"---\nname: rust-types-not-tests-lean\ndescription: \"Prefer compiler-enforced structure over tests, shell, text scans and string lists; delete before adding; no speculative generality. User called out over-engineering in the 0.20 stability batch.\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8\n  modified: 2026-11-11T21:30:02.011Z\n---\n\nIn Rust, enforce invariants with types (a trait bound, a private constructor, an exhaustive `match`) so the compiler checks them. A test is the fallback for what types cannot see, and it should be small.\n\n**Why:** In the 0.20 stability batch (2026-11-11) I let implementer agents add shell scripts plus text files to pin Action inputs, a source-text scan for `deny_unknown_fields`, a 21-invocation binary test for `schemaVersion` that the `Report` trait already guaranteed, a 233-code string list to classify exit codes, a Windows-length pathspec budget for an unsupported platform, and 15 new error codes (each a permanent frozen API). The user said \"you're not thinking in the rust way and you're over-engineering way too much\". The repo had already deleted a source-grepping test (#488).\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Before adding a test, ask whether a type or a private function makes the bug unrepresentable; do that instead. Reuse what is already in the tree (`insta`, `yaml-rust2`, `emit_report`/`Report`) rather than shell or text files.\n- No source-text scanning tests, no hand-maintained lists of strings that mirror code, no wall-clock assertions (count calls instead).\n- No code for unsupported platforms or hypothetical limits (docs/stability.md says Windows is unsupported).\n- Every new public surface (an error code, a JSON key, a flag) is permanent after 1.0: prefer fewer, broader ones.\n- Put \"prefer types, delete before adding, no speculative generality\" in every implementer brief, and review agent output for these before pushing.\n\n**Follow-up (2026-11-11):** the cleanup shipped as #508-#515 (exit_kind, one error-code registry test, pathspec chunking removed, AGENTS.md \"Enforcing an invariant\"). Verify audit claims before deleting: an auditor said clippy `map_err_ignore` made a scan test redundant, but it only flags bare `|_|`, so the test stayed; a `DryRun` newtype would have been a net addition (41 call sites), so skip when the type fix is bigger than the check. Agents' `%G?` reads `E` in the sandbox: confirm signatures with `gh api .../commits/<sha> --jq .commit.verification.reason`. The duplicate workspace load is deliberate for the validate flows; `note_once` stays.\n\nRelated: [[feedback_no_overengineering_confirm_intent]], [[feedback_measure_value_before_building]].\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":5,"oldLines":7,"newStart":5,"newLines":7,"lines":["   node_type: memory","   type: feedback","   originSessionId: 6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","-  modified: 2026-11-11T21:30:02.011Z","+  modified: 2026-11-12T01:18:14.415Z"," ---"," "," In Rust, enforce invariants with types (a trait bound, a private constructor, an exhaustive `match`) so the compiler checks them. A test is the fallback for what types cannot see, and it should be small."]},{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":4,"newStart":21,"newLines":6,"lines":[" "," **Follow-up (2026-11-11):** the cleanup shipped as #508-#515 (exit_kind, one error-code registry test, pathspec chunking removed, AGENTS.md \"Enforcing an invariant\"). Verify audit claims before deleting: an auditor said clippy `map_err_ignore` made a scan test redundant, but it only flags bare `|_|`, so the test stayed; a `DryRun` newtype would have been a net addition (41 call sites), so skip when the type fix is bigger than the check. Agents' `%G?` reads `E` in the sandbox: confirm signatures with `gh api .../commits/<sha> --jq .commit.verification.reason`. The duplicate workspace load is deliberate for the validate flows; `note_once` stays."," ","+**Again (2026-11-12):** I launched PR #529, 8 insta snapshots (2,800 lines, 95 KB) of every report schema, because an audit ranked \"no golden for report shapes\" as 1.0 blocker #1; the user called it over-engineering and I closed it. An audit's ranked list is a lead, not a mandate: before launching a build agent, ask what concrete failure it prevents, who is hurt (the owner is the only user), and what it costs on every later change (it already conflicted with #526). Prefer a compact check or nothing.","+"," Related: [[feedback_no_overengineering_confirm_intent]], [[feedback_measure_value_before_building]]."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"645dd58d-c7b1-46f5-a280-5f112ec4752a","context"