{"parentUuid":"a4e84ff6-2a11-4107-9e5e-a09c63789dc2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"3ac78390-5142-405a-ace3-2b6b7014ac74","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PWD6R8PJKKj76cqQ1y9Hua","type":"tool_result","content":"Update project intent and lifecycle\n\nUsage:\n  entire trail update [<trail>] [flags]\n\nFlags:\n      --add-assignee strings      Add assignee logins\n      --assignee strings          Assignee logins; replaces the complete list (--assignee= clears it)\n      --body string               Trail description (--body= clears it on update)\n      --branch string             Discover the project trail through this repository branch (defaults to current branch when no trail is given)\n  -h, --help                      help for update\n      --json                      Output raw JSON instead of a table\n      --priority string           Priority (urgent, high, medium, low, none)\n      --remove-assignee strings   Remove assignee logins\n      --status string             Lifecycle status (open, closed)\n      --title string              Trail title\n      --type string               Type (bug, feature, task)\n\nGlobal Flags:\n      --context name     Act as this saved login name for this command only — including the git and agent processes it spawns — instead of the active context (entire auth contexts lists them)\n      --project string   Project as gh/<owner> or et/<project>; required for list, otherwise defaults to the repository's namespace\n      --repo string      Target repository as forge/owner/repo (e.g. gh/acme/app) or a clone URL; for list, only filters the required --project\nCreate a repository-scoped project trail, then link its branch. In the current repository, publish the local branch first; no separate commit or push is required. Uses the current feature branch, or derives a new branch from the title when on the base branch. Use --branch to choose a branch, or --no-branch for intent only. With --no-branch, the current or --repo repository becomes the Trail scope and requires repository write access; --project without --repo creates unscoped intent instead. Repository writers can create scoped trails and link branches. Unscoped creation requires project management. With a branch, --idempotency-key applies only to linking; if linking fails, retry with trail link rather than creating another trail. With --repo, no local branch is published: link existing remote work or use --branch-action create to create it on the server from --base. This command does not commit changes or change the local checkout. Branches are preserved if creation fails.\n\nUsage:\n  entire trail create [flags]\n\nFlags:\n      --assignee strings         Assignee logins; replaces the complete list (--assignee= clears it)\n      --base string              Base branch for new code work\n      --body string              Trail description (--body= clears it on update)\n      --branch string            Branch to use (defaults to the current feature branch; create derives one from the title on the base branch)\n      --branch-action string     Branch action: link existing remote work, or create a new branch (default \"link\")\n  -h, --help                     help for create\n      --idempotency-key string   Retry key for linking a branch; with intent-only creation, requires project management\n      --json                     Output raw JSON instead of a table\n      --no-branch                Create intent scoped to the current or --repo repository; --project alone creates unscoped intent\n      --priority string          Priority (urgent, high, medium, low, none)\n      --status string            Lifecycle status (open, closed)\n      --title string             Trail title\n      --type string              Type (bug, feature, task)\n\nGlobal Flags:\n      --context name     Act as this saved login name for this command only — including the git and agent processes it spawns — instead of the active context (entire auth contexts lists them)\n      --project string   Project as gh/<owner> or et/<project>; required for list, otherwise defaults to the repository's namespace\n      --repo string      Target repository as forge/owner/repo (e.g. gh/acme/app) or a clone URL; for list, only filters the required --project","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"28d302cf-f143-41e1-a98f-c97250423a90","timestamp":"2026-11-12T01:30:57.628Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"Update project intent and lifecycle\n\nUsage:\n  entire trail update [<trail>] [flags]\n\nFlags:\n      --add-assignee strings      Add assignee logins\n      --assignee strings          Assignee logins; replaces the complete list (--assignee= clears it)\n      --body string               Trail description (--body= clears it on update)\n      --branch string             Discover the project trail through this repository branch (defaults to current branch when no trail is given)\n  -h, --help                      help for update\n      --json                      Output raw JSON instead of a table\n      --priority string           Priority (urgent, high, medium, low, none)\n      --remove-assignee strings   Remove assignee logins\n      --status string             Lifecycle status (open, closed)\n      --title string              Trail title\n      --type string               Type (bug, feature, task)\n\nGlobal Flags:\n      --context name     Act as this saved login name for this command only — including the git and agent processes it spawns — instead of the active context (entire auth contexts lists them)\n      --project string   Project as gh/<owner> or et/<project>; required for list, otherwise defaults to the repository's namespace\n      --repo string      Target repository as forge/owner/repo (e.g. gh/acme/app) or a clone URL; for list, only filters the required --project\nCreate a repository-scoped project trail, then link its branch. In the current repository, publish the local branch first; no separate commit or push is required. Uses the current feature branch, or derives a new branch from the title when on the base branch. Use --branch to choose a branch, or --no-branch for intent only. With --no-branch, the current or --repo repository becomes the Trail scope and requires repository write access; --project without --repo creates unscoped intent instead. Repository writers can create scoped trails and link branches. Unscoped creation requires project management. With a branch, --idempotency-key applies only to linking; if linking fails, retry with trail link rather than creating another trail. With --repo, no local branch is published: link existing remote work or use --branch-action create to create it on the server from --base. This command does not commit changes or change the local checkout. Branches are preserved if creation fails.\n\nUsage:\n  entire trail create [flags]\n\nFlags:\n      --assignee strings         Assignee logins; replaces the complete list (--assignee= clears it)\n      --base string              Base branch for new code work\n      --body string              Trail description (--body= clears it on update)\n      --branch string            Branch to use (defaults to the current feature branch; create derives one from the title on the base branch)\n      --branch-action string     Branch action: link existing remote work, or create a new branch (default \"link\")\n  -h, --help                     help for create\n      --idempotency-key string   Retry key for linking a branch; with intent-only creation, requires project management\n      --json                     Output raw JSON instead of a table\n      --no-branch                Create intent scoped to the current or --repo repository; --project alone creates unscoped intent\n      --priority string          Priority (urgent, high, medium, low, none)\n      --status string            Lifecycle status (open, closed)\n      --title string             Trail title\n      --type string              Type (