lse reason from code). Read AGENTS.md and docs/stability.md.\nQUESTION: `--dry-run` is a GLOBAL flag (crates/callisto-cli/src/cli.rs, GlobalArgs) accepted before or after any subcommand. A 1.0-readiness audit found it shows up in the help of read-only commands (`status`, `matrix`, `release pending`, `help`, `schema`, `completions`) where it is a no-op, and flags freeze at 1.0 by accident. Find: (1) every command and what `--dry-run` actually does there (writes suppressed? output changed? nothing?), from the code (`ApplyPermit::granted_unless_dry_run(global.dry_run)` call sites and any other reads of `.dry_run`); (2) every place the repo itself passes `--dry-run` (grep .github/, docs/, README, tests, benches, the justfile) and to which command, so we know what would break if read-only commands rejected it; (3) what docs/stability.md and docs/specs/*.json promise about `--dry-run`; (4) the cheapest options with consequences for scripts: (a) leave it and document it as 'accepted everywhere, a no-op on read-only commands'; (b) keep it global but hide it from the help of commands where it does nothing (clap `hide` per command is awkward: say whether feasible without per-command arg structs); (c) move it from global to the commands that write, which makes `status --dry-run` a usage error (exit 2): list the breakage. State which you recommend for a CLI with one user pre-1.0 and why, preferring the smallest change that keeps the 1.0 surface honest.\nREPORT (under 350 words): a table of commands vs dry-run behavior, repo call sites passing it, the three options with breakage, and your recommendation. Mark anything unverified.","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8/tasks/a5f116b02cb7f5a28.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"41ecd58d-cee2-4c17-8c53-e1ea0bab533f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/agent-ac9ece