{"parentUuid":"783a6f8e-da30-4ebf-b67c-ae48bc891387","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgxViTZ87odxLp37rB33q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No, we don't tout them, and the docs agent is now fixing that.\n\n**What the README says today:** \"Cargo, npm/pnpm, and PyPI monorepos\" and \"tags, GitHub releases, and assets\". Nothing more.\n\n**What exists but isn't in the README**\n- **Supply chain:** npm provenance is documented only in `docs/publishing.md`, and release-artifact attestations exist in the code and the generated workflows but in no user-facing doc I found.\n- **Language support:** native-extension platform packages (napi-rs, maturin) and which package managers are really supported aren't stated anywhere a newcomer would look.\n- **Release behavior:** the reviewable release PR (merging it is the approval), the hold state for stale PRs, safe reruns that adopt already-published versions, `--dry-run` everywhere, and JSON reports with a stable `schemaVersion`.\n\n**What I've asked the agent to do:**\n1. It will audit the README and the guides against the current binary and code, and fix what's wrong, including the ARCHITECTURE.md invariants I proposed (exit classification and the typed JSON envelope).\n2. It will add one compact \"What you get\" section to the README (at most 14 lines) containing only claims it has verified in code or by running the binary. Each claim is linked to the doc that explains it, and anything it can't verify it leaves out and lists.\n\nI won't write the feature list from memory. A wrong \"we attest X\" claim in a README is worse than silence, so each line needs evidence, and the report will show the evidence in a table. One thing I'd want you to check when it comes back: how we describe attestation, since it's a security claim.\n\nneeds input: none. I'll bring back the audit and the README section for review before any PR opens."}],"container":null,"stop_rea