{"parentUuid":"e518d4dd-cacf-403f-9b8e-6d269c70da58","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgxXQEiwLkrmTBkLwAtp7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TcCMAPwbAe8PF7fqqprZ7G","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"ae55a48dbafe514c8","summary":"Expand the README features into a fuller verified section","message":"The 'What you get' section is too thin for users: I capped it at 14 lines and 8 bullets, so it only lists what was cheap to verify. Lift the cap. On the same branch docs/catch-up-with-0-20 (PR #532 is open, add commits; do not force-push), replace that section with a fuller one: aim for roughly 40-70 lines, grouped under short bold or ### sub-headings, plain claims, no marketing adjectives, no emoji, each group 3-6 bullets, each bullet linking the doc that explains it. Keep the README readable for a newcomer deciding whether to adopt: lead each group with what the user gets, not the mechanism. Groups and candidate features to verify (include a claim ONLY if verified in code, in `callisto <cmd> --help`, or by running it on a fixture; list everything you could not verify in your report): (1) Versioning from changesets: the `.changeset/*.md` format compatible with @changesets/cli; `callisto add`, `status` (and `status --check` as a CI gate), `version`; dependency-aware cascade across the workspace with the config knobs in docs/config.md (`[cascade]` mode out-of-range|always, bump-severity, peer-escalation, preserve-npm-ranges); did-you-mean suggestions for mistyped package names; lockfile refresh. (2) Control over what moves together: `[[fixed-group]]` (lock-step versions) and `[[linked-group]]`; per-package and glob overrides (`[[package]]`, `[[package-set]]`: release-trigger, publish-to, tag-template, previous-tag-templates, changelog path); private or alternate registries (`[registries]`, kinds cargo/npm/pypi, publish timeouts) . (3) Beyond changesets: release-trigger `auto` infers bumps from conventional commits, with the pre-1.0 `pre-major-inference` policy; prerelease mode (`pre enter` / `pre exit`); throwaway `snapshot` versions; changelog layouts (`[changelog]` grouped|flat) with compare links and layered notes. (4) Releasing: what `callisto release` publishes and the order (platform packages before owners, per-distribution ordering); tags and GitHub releases; product binaries declared with `[release]` / `[[release.artifact]]` and target triples; the one release PR with a 'Merging will publish' plan; confirmation that a published version is visible before tagging; safe reruns; the supply-chain facts you already verified. (5) CI and automation: `callisto init` scaffolds the workflows (what exactly it generates for simple and matrix cases); the three composite actions `callisto-action`, `setup-callisto`, `callisto-validate` and what each does (read .github/actions/*/action.yml, mention only the documented-as-supported inputs, not the ones marked Internal); how a repo adopts it. (6) Scripting and stability: `--format json` on every command, `schemaVersion`, camelCase, exit codes 0/1/2, error codes documented in docs/errors.md, `callisto schema`, stability promises in docs/stability.md; performance only if you measure it yourself on a generated fixture (state the size and the time you got). (7) Platform and install: operating systems and architectures the release binaries cover (read the README install section and the release workflow matrix), and any unsupported platform. THEN add a short honest 'Limits' list (max 8 bullets) of what Callisto does not do or only partly does, each verified: e.g. cross-language dependencies are not inferred; maturin gets no generated workflow; Python versions must be static (PEP 621) or Poetry; Windows; only Cargo/npm/PyPI; OIDC trusted publishing is planned not built (docs/projects/OIDC-TRUSTED-PUBLISHING.md); nuget appears in publish-to enums (check whether it is actually implemented or only reserved, and say so). Do not claim comparisons with other tools. Update the hero line/lead paragraph only if a sentence there is now wrong. Verification as before: `just doc-check`, `just pre-push`, `just lint` exit 0, signed commit(s) (retry with dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true if gpg fails in the sandbox), push the same branch. REPORT (under 300 words): the claim table (claim, evidence, verified yes/no) for the NEW claims only, the list of unverified items, and any README statement you found wrong.","type":"message","recipient":"ae55a48dbafe514c8","recipient_kind":"agent","content":"The 'What you get' section is too thin for users:…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3542,"cache_read_input_tokens":855295,"output_tokens":2374,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":788},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3542,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2374,"cache_read_input_tokens":855295,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3542,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3542},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TcCMAPwbAe8PF7fqqprZ7G":{"to":"ae55a48dbafe514c8","summary":"Expand the README features into a fuller verified section","message":"The 'What you get' section is too thin for users: I capped it at 14 lines and 8 bullets, so it only lists what was cheap to verify. Lift the cap. On the same branch docs/catch-up-with-0-20 (PR #532 is open, add commits; do not force-push), replace that section with a fuller one: aim for roughly 40-70 lines, grouped under short bold or ### sub-headings, plain claims, no marketing adjectives, no emoji,