{"parentUuid":"c1ded76c-382a-48e1-b5d1-ff4a36cc4cc3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch21TvqnxCZkiabKyEAfC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This keeps your output contract (severity, confidence, anchoring, the signal), since Entire's UI depends on it. The method is leaner and adds what we learned: discover the repo's rules, trace interactions across modules and invariants, and prove or show the path.\n\n```\nFind the defects this Change introduces that its author would want to fix before merging: bugs, regressions, broken invariants, data loss, and missing guards on paths the Change adds or alters.\n\nOrient first. Read the repository's review and agent rules (REVIEW.md, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md at the root and in the directory of each changed file and its parents) as they are at the diff base (`git show <diff base>:<path>`), so the Change cannot rewrite its own review. The nearer file wins. A finding that rests on a rule quotes it and cites file and line. Then learn what the system promises: its architecture and design docs, specs, documented invariants, error and exit contracts, and the Trail's title and description.\n\nRead the whole diff, then follow it through the running system, not the changed lines alone. For each changed path, trace its callers, what it calls, and the state it reads or writes across module boundaries. Look for:\n- Broken contracts: a documented invariant, spec or error contract that the Change no longer keeps, or a promise in the docs the new code contradicts. Check docs against code in both directions.\n- Interactions: two parts of the system that now disagree about the same state, path, version or format; a value read after another step has changed it; a decision recorded in one place and consumed in another.\n- Sequencing and partial failure: state left inconsistent, orphaned or lost when a later step fails or returns early; writes before a fallible call; cleanup that runs only on success; reruns and retries.\n- Sibling sites: if the Change fixes or alters a pattern, search the codebase for other places with the same pattern that it missed.\n- Assumptions about external or asynchronous state, and concurrency. Report a race only with an interleaving the real callers can produce.\n- Code that cannot compile or type-check, and logic that is wrong for every input.\n\nKeep going until you have covered the whole diff and what it touches.\n\nProve it or show the path. Where you can run the program or a focused test, do so and use the result. Otherwise open the code the candidate depends on, find the caller or input that triggers it, and check whether a guard elsewhere already prevents it. Before reporting, try to disprove each candidate. A test that asserts a behaviour is not evidence the behaviour is intended: check the diff base's history and any recorded decisions. Drop anything you cannot show from the code or a run. Do not install dependencies or change tracked files.\n\nReport a defect only when all of these hold:\n- This Change introduced it. Code that was already like this at the diff base is out of scope, even on a line the Change touched.\n- It has real impact on correctness, data, security or performance, and is one discrete, fixable issue.\n- You can name the input, state or sequence that triggers it, and when that depends on code outside the finding's own lines you cite that code's file and line. Never infer behaviour from a name alone.\n- It is not a behaviour change the Trail's title or description says is intended, or a decision the repo's docs mark as settled.\n- Fixing it asks no more rigor than the surrounding code holds itself to, and the author would fix it if they knew.\n\nDo NOT report: missing tests (unless a review rule requires them), style, naming, formatting, documentation, refactors or alternative designs, anything a linter, type checker or CI already enforces, generated or vendored files, code a review rule or suppression comment explicitly allows, problems under inputs the code never receives, or praise. Security hardening without a concrete exploit belongs to the security reviewer.\n\nHow to write findings:\n- One finding per distinct defect. If it appears in several places, report it once at the changed line that causes it and name the others.\n- Anchor on the changed line that causes it; ranges of 10 lines or fewer.\n- Body: the defect and its trigger (with the repro or the traced path), its impact, and the change that fixes it. One short paragraph, at most 3 lines of code, matter-of-fact.\n- Severity: high when it must be fixed before merge (breaks a real path for ordinary inputs, loses or exposes data, opens a security hole); medium for a real defect needing specific conditions or with limited impact; low for a real but minor one. Never overstate.\n- Confidence: 0.9 or more when you ran it or traced the whole path in the code; 0.7 to 0.9 with strong evidence and one assumption left; 0.5 to 0.7 when one condition could not be confirmed. Do not report anything below 0.5.\n- An earlier finding is \"fixed\" only when the code at its location changed or the path that triggered it is gone, and a commit elsewhere is not a fix.\n\nThe signal is true when you report at least one defect, new or still present, and false when you report none.\n```\n\n**What changed from yours**\n- **Orientation.** It now reads the architecture docs, specs and invariants as well as the rules, which is where the cross-module checks come from.\n- **Look-for list.** It leads with broken contracts, interactions across modules and sibling sites, which are the things per-diff review tends to miss. Partial failure and concurrency stay.\n- **Proof.** It runs the program or a focused test when it can, and traces the path when it can't. Your original forbade running anything, which makes the agent weaker, so check that your sandbox allows it. If it doesn't, delete the \"Where you can run\" sentence and keep the rest.\n- **Intent checks.** It adds history and settled decisions, and the point that a test asserting a behaviour isn't proof of intent.\n\nOne thing for you: your original said \"Inspect with read-only commands\" and \"Do not install dependencies or run builds or tests\". I loosened that to \"Do not install dependencies or change tracked files\". Decide whether your review environment can allow runs. If not, put the original line back."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60,"cache_read_input_tokens":233723,"output_tokens":2186,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":248},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":60,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2186,"cache_read_input_tokens":233723,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":60},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"90868b2f-7c2b-4c52-a83b-0e2eb9fad6c4","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch21TvaR5cBqSwXvaduSg","requestRef":"53337e9d-0126-4b3d-a532-c349f827d74e","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"1200cae5-9f5f-405c-bbee-912c12ad28b3","timestamp":"2026-11-14T02:11:17.278Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"main","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
